    <name>
      <text>Determine sine and cosine from the unit circle</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Use the unit circle to determine the exact values of the following trigonometric ratios for the angle \(\theta={@theta@}^\circ\).
</p>
<p>1. \(\cos\theta=\) [[input:ans1]]
   [[validation:ans1]]

2. \(\sin\theta=\) [[input:ans2]]
   [[validation:ans2]]
</p>
<p>
Give your answers in exact form. Do not use decimal approximations.</p>
<p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution </h3>

<p><strong>Step 1: Understand the unit circle</strong></p>

<p>The unit circle has radius 1 and is centred at the origin. For an angle \(\theta\) measured from the positive \(x\)-axis, the corresponding point on the unit circle has coordinates</p>

<p>\((x,y)=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)\).</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Identify the angle</strong></p>

<p>The given angle is \(\theta={@theta@}^\circ\).</p>

<p>Use the standard angles on the unit circle to identify the corresponding coordinates.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Determine cosine</strong></p>

<p>The cosine of the angle is the \(x\)-coordinate of the corresponding point on the unit circle.</p>

<p>\(\cos\theta={@cos_theta@}\)</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Determine sine</strong></p>

<p>The sine of the angle is the \(y\)-coordinate of the corresponding point on the unit circle.</p>

<p>\(\sin\theta={@sin_theta@}\)</p>

<p><strong>Final answers</strong></p>

<p>\(\cos({@theta@}^\circ)={@cos_theta@}\)</p>

<p>\(\sin({@theta@}^\circ)={@sin_theta@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
